TI  - News Validator: A Multimodal, Confidence-Aware Framework for Real-Time Fake News Detection Using OCR, NLP, and Large Language Models
AB  - The ever-expanding growth of social media and webbased news media has dramatically increased the accessibility of false news in society. Fake news may induce widespread public anxiety, significantly affect the creation of political policy and cause millions of dollars of financial loss. Due to the enormous quantity of news stories generated online every minute, human verification of online facts is impractical. In this research paper, we propose a new detection system, “News Validator” capable of automatically detecting whether news contents (text/image) are Real or Fake news by using Artificial Intelligence(AI) protocols. News Validator combines the power of Natural Language Processing (NLP), Optical Character Recognition (OCR) and Large Language Models (i.e., machine learning protocols) to detect indications of untrue/faulty content, (e.g., expressive language, indicators of distortion and / or fraudulence (via computer vision analysis) and finally, evaluate the credibility of the source of the news content. News Validator would be able to present an accurate, factual interpretation of whether or not a news item is considered “Real” or “Fake” in real-time and offer a contextual score (confidence indicator) for each news story, along with an intuitive interface, enabling the consumer to decide whether or not to disseminate the news story further, based on News Validator results. Furthermore, our research shows that News Validator can significantly increase accuracy in detection of erroneous content and maintain integrity in the dissemination of information over digital platforms. Index Terms—Automated Detection of Fake News; Artificial Intelligence; Detection Using Machine Learning; Natural Language Processing; Optical Character Recognition; Large Language Models; Detecting Misinformation
