TI  - Generative artificial intelligence in international trade documentation: Efficiency gains, hallucination risks, and compliance liability for export-import enterprises
AB  - Generative artificial intelligence — large language models capable of producing human-like text from natural language prompts — is being adopted by export-import enterprises to draft trade documents including bills of lading, certificates of origin, packing lists, and customs declarations. The efficiency gains are real: processing times fall, labour costs shrink, and document turnaround accelerates. But so are the risks. Generative AI systems are prone to hallucination — the confident generation of plausible-sounding but factually incorrect content — and in trade documentation, a hallucinated port code, an invented tariff classification, or a fabricated regulatory approval number can trigger customs delays, financial penalties, and compliance liability cascading through the supply chain. This research examined both sides of the ledger: the efficiency benefits and the hallucination risks of generative AI in international trade documentation, as experienced by export-import enterprises in Thailand. A mixed-methods design combined a controlled document-processing experiment with 192 trade documents (64 processed manually, 64 with AI assistance, and 64 by AI alone), supplemented by semi-structured interviews with 16 trade compliance officers. Document processing times were measured, and all AI-generated outputs were audited for factual accuracy against source shipping data. Results showed that AI-assisted processing reduced median document completion time by 67.6% (from 42.6 to 13.8 minutes) and AI-only processing by 83.6% (to 7.0 minutes). However, the AI-only group contained factual errors in 23.4% of documents, compared to 4.7% in the AI-assisted group and 6.3% in the manual group. Hallucination types included fabricated HS tariff codes (8 instances), incorrect port designations (6 instances), and invented certificate numbers (4 instances). A correlation matrix revealed that document complexity was the strongest predictor of hallucination frequency (r = 0.74, p < 0.001). Interviews with compliance officers identified three liability concerns: regulatory accountability for AI-generated errors, insurability of AI-related compliance failures, and the absence of industry standards for AI use in statutory trade documents. A cycle diagram of the AI documentation workflow was developed, marking the points at which hallucination risk is highest and human verification is most needed. The research proposes a
