TI  - FedProc-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for Federal Procurement NLP, and a Compact 180M-Parameter Model for Low-Hallucination Clause Extraction
AB  - Four-task benchmark (notice-type, NAICS sector, set-aside, token-level FAR/DFARS span extraction) from real SAM.gov solicitations + real eCFR clause text + validated synthetic excerpts. FedProc-180M (single ModernBERT-base encoder + 4 heads, 149M params, trained <5 min on one consumer GPU): entity F1 0.800 full test / 0.921 eval slice vs Claude Haiku 4.5 0.804; hallucination on real regulatory text 13.8% vs 32.1% Haiku and 11.0% GPT-4o, at fraction of latency/cost. Honest report of SAM.gov rate-limit constraint depressing 2 of 4 tasks. Files: rejected-research.pdf = manuscript (10 pages). Earlier submission arXiv:submit/7913776 [cs.CL] 5 Aug 2026, still on hold. Retained as benchmark + small-model record; ledger task continued in FedProc-Ledger v1 (separate Zenodo record). Abstract: Extracting structured information from U.S. federal procurement text – notice types, industry sectors, small-business set-asides, and FAR/DFARS clause citations – is a narrow but high-stakes extraction problem: a fabricated clause number is a wrong answer with legal and financial consequences. We introduce FedProc-Bench and release FedProc-180M, competitive with Claude Haiku 4.5 while producing far fewer fabricated clause numbers, and discuss what this does and does not show about small specialized models in regulated-text settings.
