TY  - RPRT
TI  - Machine Learning Validation Pipelines: From Tabular Benchmarking to Conformal Calibration and Execution-Grounded Agentic Testing
AB  - Abstract Machine learning systems are increasingly required to be multi-layered for software engineering validation protocols, and multiple checks are imposed instead of just one. Many important failure modes, such as deep class imbalance, poor probability calibration, ungrounded execution, and ”attributional” issues, come to a head in low-level deployments, such as automated code generation, credit underwriting, and clinical triage, where failures can be devastating. In clinical triage, credit underwriting, and the automated generation of code situations that are of a high stakes regularly show up critical failure modes such as severe class imbalance, poor probability calibration, unfaithful attribution, and ungrounded execution, for which standard k-fold cross-validation and regularized baselines are entirely inadequate. By combining the findings from 41 empirical studies, this review identifies three main levels of architecture: data leakage prevention and baseline benchmarking (Level 1), post-hoc interpretability audits and glass-box comparisons (Level 2), and distribution-free uncertainty quantification (Level 3). It is particularly important to note that a meta-analysis of 297 financial machine learning papers reveals a general disregard for diagnosis; only 7.1% of the papers examined included Remove and Retrain (ROAR) benchmarks for testing the faithfulness of the explanations, whereas 93.9% used TreeSHAP for feature attribution. The existence of multi-tier verification frameworks is especially important in order to prevent silent failure modes in safetycritical software systems, as these findings demonstrate. Class-conditional Mondrian conformal calibration answers the Minority Coverage Collapse Theorem, recovering +61.7 percentage points of minority coverage (p < 10 −80 ), and pairs with conformalized quantile regression and cost-optimal selective human deferral (θ ∗ = ch/CFN). Layer 4 is pre-deployment gating: on models above 0.85 AUROC, where static toolkits report nothing amiss, the RISED framework surfaces decision threshold-flip rates of 49.1% to 64.2% under minor input perturbations. Layer 5 covers execution-grounded multi-agent verification: closed-loop Abstract Syntax Tree validation with transactional rollback (ExecuGraph), lightweight multi-vector screening (TriEval), arity-matched action fairness auditing (AgentFairBench), and prompt-sensitivity benchmarking of the LLM judges such pipelines rely on to score free-form output (JudgeSense). We then formalize how the layers couple, and set out the order in which they should run.
