TI  - The Grand Convergence Model: A Three-Part Systems Framework for Structural Risk, Empirical Calibration, and Residence Exposure in Advanced Economies During the AI-Automation Era
AB  - This record contains the Grand Convergence Model working-paper series, a three-part framework for analysing structural instability in advanced economies during the AI-automation era. Working Paper No. 1 introduces the Grand Convergence Model: a scenario-based systems framework examining how automation and AI penetration, demographic contraction, financial fragility, environmental stress, geopolitical fragmentation, social cohesion, and policy capacity interact under conditions of structural transition. Working Paper No. 2 provides the empirical scaffolding for the model. It identifies observable indicators for the model’s measurable stress domains and introduces the Composite Convergence Index (CCI), a weighted monitoring instrument designed to track the accumulation of cross-domain structural pressures over time. Working Paper No. 3, Reach Without Residence, extends the framework by introducing the residence–wilting matrix, the concept of delamination risk, and the proposed Residence Gap Index (RGI). It distinguishes economies whose resident base is structurally wilting from economies whose headline activity is weakly attached to resident employment, household income, and demographic anchoring. Together, the three papers develop a unified analytical architecture for examining post-labour economic risk: first as a threshold problem, then as an empirical monitoring problem, and finally as a residence-and-brittleness problem. The purpose of the Grand Convergence Model is not to predict collapse, but to provide a systems-level analytical map for policymakers, researchers, and institutions navigating the structural transition toward post-labour economic conditions. License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0. AI Collaboration Note: Portions of the analytical development, editing, and structural refinement of these working papers were conducted with the assistance of AI language models. All conceptual design, interpretation, and final authorship remain the responsibility of the author.
